{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KfJ4h9TebyJYttRYdG9qyF","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: rel01.2-uc001-sponge\n     2→title: \"Soak stdin and write to file via sponge\"\n     3→\n     4→summary: |\n     5→  The operator runs a pipeline that reads a file, transforms it, and pipes the result through\n     6→  the Go sponge binary back to the same file. sponge reads all of stdin before opening the\n     7→  output file, making the idiom \"cmd file | sponge file\" safe. The differential testing\n     8→  harness runs both the Go binary and the Homebrew reference binary (sponge from moreutils)\n     9→  with the same stdin and arguments, then compares the output file contents byte-for-byte.\n    10→  Unlike wc and cat, sponge verification uses file-content comparison, not stdout comparison.\n    11→\n    12→actor: Human Operator\n    13→\n    14→trigger: |\n    15→  The operator has completed the PRD (prd007-sponge.yaml) and invokes do-work to produce\n    16→  a passing differential test for cmd/sponge.\n    17→\n    18→flow:\n    19→  - id: F1\n    20→    step: |\n    21→      The operator runs a pipeline that pipes file content through the Go sponge binary back\n    22→      to the same file. Example: cat data.txt | ./bin/sponge data.txt\n    23→  - id: F2\n    24→    step: |\n    25→      cmd/sponge reads all bytes from stdin into an in-memory buffer. If the buffer exceeds\n    26→      the memory threshold, sponge spills to a temp file and continues reading. The output\n    27→      file is not opened during this phase.\n    28→  - id: F3\n    29→    step: |\n    30→      After stdin reaches EOF, cmd/sponge opens the output file. If the output file already\n    31→      exists, sponge reads its permissions for later restoration. In append mode (-a), sponge\n    32→      prepends the original file content to the buffered stdin content.\n    33→  - id: F4\n    34→    step: |\n    35→      cmd/sponge writes the buffered content to a temp file, then atomically renames the temp\n    36→      file to the output path. If the rename fails (cross-device), sponge falls back to a\n    37→      byte-for-byte copy.\n    38→  - id: F5\n    39→    step: |\n    40→      The differential testing harness (pkg/testutils) executes both ./bin/sponge and the\n    41→      reference sponge binary with the same stdin, args, and environment in isolated temp\n    42→      directories.\n    43→  - id: F6\n    44→    step: |\n    45→      The harness compares the output file contents byte-for-byte using the file-state\n    46→      comparison capability (prd001-testutils R5). Both binaries must produce identical\n    47→      output file content.\n    48→  - id: F7\n    49→    step: |\n    50→      If the file contents match, the test passes. If they diverge, the harness reports the\n    51→      triggering args, both file contents, and both exit codes.\n    52→\n    53→touchpoints:\n    54→  - T1: \"cmd/sponge — soak-before-write contract, atomic rename, append mode, passthrough (prd007-sponge R1, R2, R3, R4, R5)\"\n    55→  - T2: \"pkg/testutils — RunDiffTests, file-state comparison\"\n    56→\n    57→success_criteria:\n    58→  - id: S1\n    59→    criterion: cmd/sponge compiles and ./bin/sponge exists after mage build.\n    60→  - id: S2\n    61→    criterion: |\n    62→      The soak-before-write contract holds: \"cat file | ./bin/sponge file\" produces a file\n    63→      containing the original content (not empty or truncated), confirming sponge did not\n    64→      open the output file until stdin was fully consumed.\n    65→  - id: S3\n    66→    criterion: |\n    67→      Passthrough mode works: \"printf 'hello' | ./bin/sponge\" writes 'hello' to stdout,\n    68→      matching the reference binary.\n    69→  - id: S4\n    70→    criterion: |\n    71→      Append mode works: \"printf 'appended\\n' | ./bin/sponge -a existing.txt\" produces a\n    72→      file containing [original content][appended\\n], matching the reference binary.\n    73→  - id: S5\n    74→    criterion: |\n    75→      Large input (1 MB or more) triggers temp-file spill and produces correct output file\n    76→      content, matching the reference binary.\n    77→  - id: S6\n    78→    criterion: |\n    79→      File permissions are preserved: an output file with mode 0640 retains that mode after\n    80→      sponge overwrites it.\n    81→  - id: S7\n    82→    criterion: |\n    83→      The differential test passes for all modes: small stdin, large stdin, append, passthrough,\n    84→      and soak-before-write contract.\n    85→\n    86→out_of_scope:\n    87→  - Cross-device rename fallback is covered in the test suite but is not the primary flow.\n    88→  - SIGINT/SIGTERM cleanup behavior is specified in the PRD but is not directly testable in the\n    89→    differential harness; it is verified by manual inspection.\n    90→  - Memory threshold tuning is not tested; the spill path is exercised by the large-input test.\n    91→\n    92→test_suite: test-rel01.2\n    93→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0c5e0c7b-820f-41f4-bffe-6ff0c2b25f45","uuid":"58092fa1-f301-4c4c-8b14-bf9ae74bfc23","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/122/docs/specs/use-cases/rel01.2-uc001-sponge.yaml","content":"id: rel01.2-uc001-sponge\ntitle: \"Soak stdin and write to file via sponge\"\n\nsummary: |\n  The operator runs a pipeline that reads a file, transforms it, and pipes the result through\n  the Go sponge binary back to the same file. sponge reads all of stdin before opening the\n  output file, making the idiom \"cmd file | sponge file\" safe. The differential testing\n  harness runs both the Go binary and the Homebrew reference binary (sponge from moreutils)\n  with the same stdin and arguments, then compares the output file contents byte-for-byte.\n  Unlike wc and cat, sponge verification uses file-content comparison, not stdout comparison.\n\nactor: Human Operator\n\ntrigger: |\n  The operator has completed the PRD (prd007-sponge.yaml) and invokes do-work to produce\n  a passing differential test for cmd/sponge.\n\nflow:\n  - id: F1\n    step: |\n      The operator runs a pipeline that pipes file content through the Go sponge binary back\n      to the same file. Example: cat data.txt | ./bin/sponge data.txt\n  - id: F2\n    step: |\n      cmd/sponge reads all bytes from stdin into an in-memory buffer. If the buffer exceeds\n      the memory threshold, sponge spills to a temp file and continues reading. The output\n      file is not opened during this phase.\n  - id: F3\n    step: |\n      After stdin reaches EOF, cmd/sponge opens the output file. If the output file already\n      exists, sponge reads its permissions for later restoration. In append mode (-a), sponge\n      prepends the original file content to the buffered stdin content.\n  - id: F4\n    step: |\n      cmd/sponge writes the buffered content to a temp file, then atomically renames the temp\n      file to the output path. If the rename fails (cross-device), sponge falls back to a\n      byte-for-byte copy.\n  - id: F5\n    step: |\n      The differential testing harness (pkg/testutils) executes both ./bin/sponge and the\n      reference sponge binary with the same stdin, args, and environment in isolated temp\n      directories.\n  - id: F6\n    step: |\n      The harness compares the output file contents byte-for-byte using the file-state\n      comparison capability (prd001-testutils R5). Both binaries must produce identical\n      output file content.\n  - id: F7\n    step: |\n      If the file contents match, the test passes. If they diverge, the harness reports the\n      triggering args, both file contents, and both exit codes.\n\ntouchpoints:\n  - T1: \"cmd/sponge — soak-before-write contract, atomic rename, append mode, passthrough (prd007-sponge R1, R2, R3, R4, R5)\"\n  - T2: \"pkg/testutils — RunDiffTests, file-state comparison\"\n\nsuccess_criteria:\n  - id: S1\n    criterion: cmd/sponge compiles and ./bin/sponge exists after mage build.\n  - id: S2\n    criterion: |\n      The soak-before-write contract holds: \"cat file | ./bin/sponge file\" produces a file\n      containing the original content (not empty or truncated), confirming sponge did not\n      open the output file until stdin was fully consumed.\n  - id: S3\n    criterion: |\n      Passthrough mode works: \"printf 'hello' | ./bin/sponge\" writes 'hello' to stdout,\n      matching the reference binary.\n  - id: S4\n    criterion: |\n      Append mode works: \"printf 'appended\\n' | ./bin/sponge -a existing.txt\" produces a\n      file containing [original content][appended\\n], matching the reference binary.\n  - id: S5\n    criterion: |\n      Large input (1 MB or more) triggers temp-file spill and produces correct output file\n      content, matching the reference binary.\n  - id: S6\n    criterion: |\n      File permissions are preserved: an output file with mode 0640 retains that mode after\n      sponge overwrites it.\n  - id: S7\n    criterion: |\n      The differential test passes for all modes: small stdin, large stdin, append, passthrough,\n      and soak-before-write contract.\n\nout_of_scope:\n  - Cross-device rename fallback is covered in the test suite but is not the primary flow.\n  - SIGINT/SIGTERM cleanup behavior is specified in the PRD but is not directly testable in the\n    differential harness; it is verified by manual inspection.\n  - Memory threshold tuning is not tested; the spill path is exercised by the large-input test.\n\ntest_suite: test-rel01.2\n","numLines":93,"startLine":1,"totalLines":93}}}
